From 5d0df5764a79b6e3358a0e31aeffed474a5d75d5 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: LegendaryFire <tristan.balon@outlook.com>
Date: Sat, 21 Nov 2026 15:46:26 -0700
Subject: [PATCH] Trim Inkling 2's decode VRAM, cheaper and longer DSpark
 drafts

A decode's attention partials were sized for 256-key pieces of the
whole context (~200 MiB a lane a card at 512K): a RAM part's staged
blocks are now read by k_attn_dreg in 2048-key partials (the same
tokens out as the one-pass kernel at 64K with a 32K window), the
buffer sized for that, and rows only as many as take that path. The
drafter's features are FP16 and its context rows a layer at a time.
2x512K, two lanes, DSpark: 2820 -> 2920 experts in VRAM at start.

DSpark's adaptive length never tried a length it had not measured
(its prior favoured 1 at long contexts: 86% of drafts stood, 1.9
tokens a step): every eighth step tries one more, and an unmeasured
verify row is priced at 12%, not 25%. 180K, greedy: 49.5 -> 56.1
tok/s; temperature 1: 43.4 (35.6 without drafting).

A draft read the target's 469 MiB head once a drafted token (and, a
sampled one, copied its 200K logits to the host): the draft rows'
head logits are now one read for all of them, each row's top 256
candidates picked on the card (a radix select, 48 blocks a row), and
each step scores only those (their head rows times the correction),
softcapped; greedy takes their argmax on the card, a sampled draft
draws from them on the host. The reply stays exact (the verify);
greedy output unchanged. 7K: 80.9 -> 84.6 tok/s greedy, 53.6 -> 56.1
at temperature 1; 60K: 56.4 -> 55.0 greedy, 50.5 -> 55.0.
